ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [Seeded PRNG (seed=42) producing identical output across processes is mathematically trivial — `rand.NewSource(42)` is a deterministic LCG by definition; "identical bytes across 30 processes" is the *predicted* outcome, not a discovery (CWE-330/338). Cross-language identity-of-determinism is likewise predicted: both guests draw from the same host-side seeded stream, so of course both are deterministic; the fact that Rust and Go bytes *differ* from each other is just different consumption patterns of one PRNG — not an additional finding. Clock ~31x speedup is the documented +1ms/read increment vs. ~100ms real sleeps replaced by `nanosleep`-instant; the ratio is an arithmetic consequence of the documented knobs, not an emergent effect.]
CONFOUNDS: [The "composition / abstraction-leak" framing is doing the load-bearing work, but it is not independently measured — packet provides no metric distinguishing "composition is un-owned" from "each knob is documented and a reader must compose them mentally." Un-ownership is argued from absence-of-search-hits, which is weak evidence (search recall unknown, query strings not disclosed). "Gold-standard crypto/rand" claim presumes guest authors expect WASI `random_get` to be CSPRNG-backed; this expectation itself is not measured (no survey, no doc audit of WASI spec language on `random_get` entropy guarantees).]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [(1) That `crypto/rand.Reader` in a WASI guest is contractually CSPRNG — WASI preview1 `random_get` spec language re: entropy quality not cited. (2) That real-world wazero hosts use `NewModuleConfig()` defaults for guests doing crypto (L1 prevalence explicitly deferred — so the "blast radius" is unestablished). (3) That the composition is *un-warned* — packet says "config.go documents each knob INDIVIDUALLY; NONE warns the composition" but does not quote the doc strings or show a reader could not reasonably infer the compound effect from reading any two adjacent knobs. (4) That 30 / 10 processes is sufficient sampling — for a deterministic seed the answer is trivially yes, but this means the N is theatrical, not statistical.]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [N=30 (Go) / N=10 (Rust) processes — for a *deterministic* PRNG, any N>1 is sufficient and any N is equally uninformative; no variance/error bars are meaningful here, which is fine but should be acknowledged rather than presented as "100% collision rate" (a rate over a deterministic process is not a rate). Clock ratio reported as point estimate "~31x" / "~4.8ms" with no repetition variance or scheduler-jitter bound. No measurement of how many wazero-using projects in the wild actually rely on `NewModuleConfig()` defaults — the prevalence denominator for "silent hazard" is missing at L0 (acknowledged as L1 work, but it caps the L0 claim's reach).]
RATIONALE: The L0 experiment is clean and the cross-language ABI-boundary arm does lift the finding above pure single-runtime folklore — observing that a Rust/getrandom guest is demoted identically *is* the seam-level evidence and is more than "predictable RNG bad" (cited: Rust 10/10 byte-identical 538c7f96...09dd9d52, Go 30/30 byte-identical 46a4405f...641cb876, both via the same seed-42 host stream). However, the central theoretical claim — that the composition is a *genuinely un-owned publishable hazard* rather than the documented sum of documented parts — rests on (a) absence-of-prior-art evidence whose search methodology isn't shown, and (b) an un-measured assumption that WASI `random_get` carries a CSPRNG contract that wazero's default silently violates. Without the WASI-spec citation (does preview1 mandate cryptographic-quality entropy from `random_get`? if yes, this is a spec-violation finding and stronger; if no, it's "host chose a documented non-CSPRNG default" and weaker) and without an L1 prevalence number, the composition argument is suggestive but not yet decisive over the GATE-4 folklore objection. Yellow: advance to L1 with (i) WASI preview1 `random_get` entropy-contract citation, (ii) doc-audit quoting whether any wazero surface warns the *composition*, (iii) prevalence sample of real wazero hosts' module-config patterns.
